  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 528 AM CDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nA low-amplitude ridge is building over the Northern Plains \nearly this morning ahead of a deep longwave trough digging into \nthe western CONUS. Broad surface high pressure over Manitoba/ \nOntario is providing a weak easterly upslope surface flow into \nan antecedently moisture-rich air mass, which has resulted in \nwidespread dense fog across much of western and central North \nDakota. A Dense Fog Advisory is in effect for all but far \nsouthwest portions of our forecast area. The fog should lift \nduring the mid to late morning hours as a deepening lee cyclone \nover central Montana strengthens southeasterly return flow over \nthe western Dakotas. Surface winds are forecast to increase to \naround 25 mph in western North Dakota this afternoon, with gusts\nup to 35 mph. Meanwhile, the thermal ridge will also expand \ninto western North Dakota this afternoon, bringing unseasonably \nwarm temperatures as high as the mid 90s to southwest parts of \nthe state, decreasing to more pleasant temperatures in the mid \nto upper 70s in the northeast. The combination of stronger winds\nand hot temperatures yielding RH as low as 15 percent is \nexpected to result in critical fire weather conditions in far \nsouthwest North Dakota this afternoon and early evening. A Red \nFlag Warning is in effect for Golden Valley, Slope, and Bowman \nCounties.\n\nThe latest suite of CAMs shows low probabilities for convection\ninitiating over parts of central North Dakota late tonight into\nSaturday morning in response to strong warm air and moisture \nadvection induced by the nocturnal low level jet. Despite MUCAPE\npossibly increasing above 2000 J/kg, weak effective bulk shear \nand the elevated nature of the convection keeps severe \nprobabilities very low. The upper level height ridge axis and \nunderlying thermal ridge axis will become more focused over the \nNorthern Plains on Saturday, keeping high temperatures well \nabove normal in the mid 80s to mid 90s. Shortwave energy will \nbegin ejecting from the base of the deep western CONUS trough \non Saturday. There may be a narrow window for surface-based \nconvection in southern North Dakota during the late afternoon \nand early evening hours on Saturday. Otherwise, elevated \nshowers and storms could become more prominent Saturday night. \nIn the event of surface-based convection, strong buoyancy and \nmodest deep layer shear could support a few strong to severe \nthunderstorms across southern North Dakota during the late \nafternoon and evening. Severe probabilities should diminish \nlate Saturday night, but lingering strong buoyancy could still \nsupport a pulse strong to severe storm.\n\nBroad mid level height falls in response to a potent shortwave \nejecting from the base of the western CONUS longwave trough \nkeeps medium chances for showers and thunderstorms in the \nforecast for much of western and central North Dakota during \nthe day Sunday, increasing to high chances (60 to 90 percent) \nSunday night into Labor Day as the potent shortwave lifts \nnortheast off the mountains of Montana and Wyoming. CAPE on the\norder of 2000 J/kg and effective bulk shear around 30-40 kts \nkeeps a low risk for strong to severe thunderstorms in the \nforecast for nearly all of North Dakota on Sunday. As mentioned,\nthe highest rain chances are focused from Sunday night through \nLabor Day as the upstream longwave trough begins to kick \nnortheast. NBM probabilities for at least an inch of rain over \nthe Sunday through Labor Day time period range from only around \n10 percent in far southwest North Dakota to as high as 80 to 90 \npercent along the Canadian border. This disparity appears to be \nat least partially influenced by a dry slot attendant to a \nsurface cyclone lifting northeastward through the region on \nLabor Day. The frontal boundary ushering in the dry slot could \ninitiate a conditional severe risk in a lower CAPE/higher shear \nenvironment, but forecast confidence in convective details at \nthis time range remains quite low. From Tuesday to Wednesday, \nan occlusion of the low is projected over the southern Canadian \nprairie provinces on Tuesday before it quickly jets toward the \nnorthern Great Lakes and New England. This favors drier, \nslightly cooler, and windier conditions for western and central \nNorth Dakota Tuesday and Wednesday. Flow aloft may then \ntransition to quasi-zonal by the end of next week, which could \nmoderate temperatures to near normal values and return periodic \nlower chances for rain.",
